{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"**Answer**\n\n- **Cause**  \n  The `similarity` function returned a Python `float`. When a story was published and the library already contained other stories, the computed similarity scores were stored in the `similar` attribute of the DynamoDB item. DynamoDB (and the test `FakeTable`) reject floats, raising `TypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead`. The first publish succeeded because the table was empty, so no similarity scores were written.\n\n- **Fix**  \n  Updated `src/app.py`:\n  * Added a detailed docstring explaining the issue.  \n  * After computing the weighted Jaccard score, the code now converts the result to an **integer percentage** (`int(round(raw_score * 100))`). This yields an `int` (or `Decimal`‑compatible) value, eliminating the Float‑type error.\n\n- **Applied Parts from Colleague’s Note**  \n  * Identified the same “float to DynamoDB” failure path.  \n  * Implemented the one‑line change (convert scores to ints) that the note recommended.  \n  * No additional test changes were needed because the existing test already covers publishing after the first insert."}}
